# rules: use-of-md5
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-327-md5.kt
# from: kotlin/lang/security/use-of-md5.yaml
#
# MD5 has had practical collisions since 2004 and chosen-prefix collisions
# since 2019, so two inputs with the same digest can be produced to order. A
# signature over an MD5 digest signs both of them.
#
# The rule as translated asked for `$VAR = $MD.getInstance("MD5")`, which is an
# assignment. Kotlin introduces almost every value with `val` or `var`, and a
# declaration is a different node, so the only spelling it could ever find was
# the Apache DigestUtils one - and the line every program actually writes went
# unreported, silently, for as long as the rule existed.
#
# The fix is to stop naming the binding at all. `$MD.getInstance($ALGO)` is the
# call wherever it stands, inside a declaration or an assignment or on its own,
# and it is what the rule was always about. Where a rule in this corpus names a
# binding it should be because the binding is part of the question.#
# The helpers are written with their argument, and the argument is what tells
# them from okio's. `ByteString.md5()` takes none and is how a Kotlin program
# spells a cache key: OkHttp's own `Cache.key` is
# `url.toString().encodeUtf8().md5().hex()`, which is a real MD5 and is not a
# weakness in anything. A pattern that named the method and not the arity
# reported it, and there is nothing in the syntax that separates a cache key
# from a signature. So the argument-less spelling is deliberately not matched,
# which costs the rare program that hashes a password that way and keeps the
# rule quiet on the many that do not.

["$MD.getInstance($ALGO)"] as $made
| ["$DU.getMd5Digest().digest($$$_)", "DigestUtils.md5($X)",
   "DigestUtils.md5Hex($X)", "$DU.md5Hex($X)"] as $helpers
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $made + $helpers) as $all
| ( ($all | of($made) | where_capture("ALGO"; "^\"MD5\"$"))
    + ($all | of($helpers)) )
| finding("use-of-md5";
    "MD5 has had chosen-prefix collisions since 2019, so two inputs with this "
    + "digest can be produced to order; SHA-256 instead")
| report
